("Battle of Yarmouk",636,"August 636","Battle","Syria","Opened the Levant to the Arabs","Arab Muslim forces under Khalid ibn al-Walid destroyed a large Byzantine army in a six-day battle on the Yarmouk river.","Exhausted by its war with Persia, the Byzantine army was divided by ethnic rivalries; Arab cavalry exploited a gap and drove them into a ravine. Emperor Heraclius reportedly said 'Farewell, Syria'. Jerusalem surrendered in 637, and the Levant became Muslim and Arabic-speaking — reshaping the Middle East permanently.","Battle_of_the_Yarmuk","Middle East","Medieval world"),
("Battle of Palkhed",1728,"28 February 1728","Battle","Maharashtra","Maratha mastery of the Deccan","Bajirao I outmanoeuvred the Nizam of Hyderabad without a major battle.","Bajirao's light cavalry raided the Nizam's territory, drawing his heavy army after him, and then trapped it without water near Palkhed, forcing surrender. It is studied as a model of mobile warfare, and established Maratha supremacy in the Deccan, leading to their expansion north.","Battle_of_Palkhed","South Asia","Early modern world"),
("Battle of Jamrud",1837,"30 April 1837","Battle","Khyber Pass","Sikh hold on the Khyber","Afghan forces attacked the Sikh fort at Jamrud; Hari Singh Nalwa was killed, but the fort held.","The battle stopped Afghan attempts to retake Peshawar, keeping the Khyber in Sikh hands until the British annexed Punjab.","Battle_of_Jamrud","South Asia","Early modern world"),
("Battle of Waterloo (battle detail)",1815,"18 June 1815","Battle","Belgium","Ended Napoleon's rule","See Battle of Waterloo.","—","Battle_of_Waterloo","Europe","Early modern world"),
